# Optional: switch storage from the default SQLite file (./data/docuwaves.db)
# to PostgreSQL. Only takes effect together with `docker compose --profile
# postgres up -d` (see docker-compose.yml). Leave blank to use SQLite -- either
# way, this is only ever a rebuildable search/browse INDEX, see CONTENT_REPO_URL
# below for where the actual content lives.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://docuwaves:changeme@postgres:5432/docuwaves

# Optional: single sign-on via any standard OIDC provider (Authentik,
# Keycloak, Authelia, Zitadel, ...). Leave all three blank to disable --
# the password login form is always available regardless.
# OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://auth.example.com/application/o/docuwaves/
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=authentik

# Content repo -- OPTIONAL, and everything in this file is. DocuWaves keeps
# all content as Markdown+YAML files in a Git repository (see the README's
# "Content repo structure" for the layout), and with CONTENT_REPO_URL blank
# that repository is a local one it creates for itself under
# CONTENT_REPO_PATH, inside the data volume. That is a complete setup: every
# save is a real commit, with full history, diffs, restore and attribution.
# No account anywhere, no token, nothing to configure.
#
# NOTE, for that case: the data volume is then the ONLY copy of your
# documentation. Back up ./data (or set a remote below).
#
# Setting CONTENT_REPO_URL gives that repository a REMOTE -- somewhere to
# push, and somewhere a community can send pull requests from. Point it at
# an EMPTY repository (GitHub/GitLab/your own Forgejo/Gitea/anything) and
# the history you already have is pushed there in full on the next start;
# nothing you wrote locally is lost or flattened. Removing it again puts the
# instance back into local mode, likewise without losing anything. See the
# README's "Adding a Git remote later", including what happens if the remote
# turns out NOT to be empty.
#
# HTTPS remote (needs a token with push access, e.g. a GitHub fine-grained
# PAT scoped to just this one repo, Contents: Read and write):
# CONTENT_REPO_URL=https://github.com/your-org/your-docs-content.git
# CONTENT_REPO_TOKEN=
#
# SSH remote instead (needs a deploy key with write access; paste the
# PRIVATE key's full contents, including the ----BEGIN/END---- lines):
# CONTENT_REPO_URL=git@github.com:your-org/your-docs-content.git
# CONTENT_REPO_SSH_KEY=
#
# Optional: the address readers actually use, e.g. https://docs.example.com.
# Only needed as an OVERRIDE -- every absolute URL DocuWaves publishes
# (canonical tags, Open Graph, sitemap.xml, robots.txt) is otherwise built
# from X-Forwarded-Proto/X-Forwarded-Host and the Host header, which is right
# behind any ordinary reverse proxy. Set it if your proxy doesn't forward
# those, or if the site answers at several addresses and one of them is the
# canonical one. Check what's being used with:
#   curl -s https://your-host/robots.txt | tail -1
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://docs.example.com
#
# Optional: which header carries the reader's real address. Only needed with
# MORE than one proxy in front of DocuWaves (e.g. Cloudflare in front of your
# nginx) -- behind a single reverse proxy the rightmost X-Forwarded-For entry
# is used, which that proxy sets itself. It feeds the rate limits on sign-in,
# the chat and page feedback, so a wrong value lets everyone share one limit.
# CLIENT_IP_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP
#
# Optional: how many chat questions may wait on the model at once (default 4).
# CHAT_MAX_CONCURRENT=4
#
# Optional: the gaps radar -- searches that found nothing, tallied (words and
# counts only, never who searched; 90 days). On unless switched off.
# SEARCH_GAPS=off
#
# Optional: send the admin session cookie over HTTPS only. Default: on when
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL is an https:// address, off otherwise (plain HTTP on a LAN).
# Set it to true behind any HTTPS reverse proxy that doesn't set PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true
#
# Optional: tell a Discord/Slack channel or any JSON endpoint when a page is
# published, updated or taken down. Several URLs separated by commas. A
# Discord or Slack webhook URL is itself the password to post there -- keep it
# out of chats and screenshots.
# WEBHOOK_URLS=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/...
# Which events to send: published (default), updated, unpublished.
# WEBHOOK_EVENTS=published,updated
# Signs plain JSON deliveries (header X-DocuWaves-Signature: sha256=<hmac>).
# WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Branch to track (default: main) -- also the branch a local repository is
# initialised on -- and how often the background sync job pulls + reindexes
# on its own, in seconds (default: every 5 minutes). The sync job only runs
# with a remote configured: a local instance is the sole writer of its own
# repository, so there is nothing for it to find. There's also a "Sync now"
# button in the admin UI for an immediate pull, shown for the same reason
# only when there is a remote.
# CONTENT_REPO_BRANCH=main
# CONTENT_REPO_SYNC_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# Optional: a code the first-run setup screen asks for before it creates the
# first admin account -- so that on a server the internet can reach, nobody
# who finds the address before you can claim the instance. Something long and
# random (`openssl rand -hex 16`); the installer sets one for you. Unused once
# an account exists.
# SETUP_TOKEN=
